issues.body:
## Problem Description

The Research page (`/uk/research` or `/us/research`) loads all ~160 blog post card images simultaneously on page load. This causes:

1. **Visible loading delays** - Images at the top of the page remain invisible while competing with off-screen images for bandwidth
2. **Poor perceived performance** - Users see empty gray placeholder boxes while waiting
3. **Unnecessary bandwidth usage** - Off-screen images are loaded even if the user never scrolls to them
4. **Potential blocking of critical resources** - Many concurrent image requests can impact other page resources

## Current Implementation

**File:** `app/src/components/blog/BlogPostCard.tsx` (lines 78-92)

```tsx
{item.image && (
  <img
    src={`/assets/posts/${item.image}`}
    alt={item.title}
    style={{
      width: '100%',
      height: '100%',
      objectFit: 'cover',
    }}
    onError={(e) => {
      e.currentTarget.style.display = 'none';
    }}
  />
)}
```

**Issues:**
- No `loading="lazy"` attribute
- No `width`/`height` attributes (causes layout shift)
- No `decoding="async"` attribute
- All images load immediately regardless of viewport position

**Note:** The blog post content images in `MarkdownFormatter.tsx` already have `loading="lazy"` - this fix should bring card images in line with that pattern.

## Recommended Solution

### Option 1: Native Lazy Loading (Simplest - Recommended First Step)

Add native browser lazy loading attributes to `BlogPostCard.tsx`:

```tsx
<img
  src={`/assets/posts/${item.image}`}
  alt={item.title}
  loading="lazy"
  decoding="async"
  width={400}
  height={200}
  style={{
    width: '100%',
    height: '100%',
    objectFit: 'cover',
  }}
  onError={(e) => {
    e.currentTarget.style.display = 'none';
  }}
/>
```

**Pros:**
- Zero dependencies
- Excellent browser support (95%+ coverage)
- Minimal code change
- Browser handles loading optimization automatically

**Cons:**
- Less control over loading thresholds
- No callback hooks for loading states

### Option 2: Intersection Observer (If More Control Needed)

Use `react-intersection-observer` for finer control:

```tsx
import { useInView } from 'react-intersection-observer';

function BlogPostCard({ item, countryId }: BlogPostCardProps) {
  const { ref, inView } = useInView({
    triggerOnce: true,
    rootMargin: '200px', // Start loading 200px before visible
  });

  return (
    <Box ref={ref}>
      {/* ... */}
      <Box style={{ height: '200px', /* ... */ }}>
        {item.image && inView && (
          <img
            src={`/assets/posts/${item.image}`}
            alt={item.title}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            width={400}
            height={200}
            style={{ /* ... */ }}
          />
        )}
      </Box>
      {/* ... */}
    </Box>
  );
}
```

**Pros:**
- Control over when loading begins (rootMargin)
- Can show placeholder/skeleton while loading
- Can trigger animations on visibility
- Useful for analytics (track which cards are viewed)

**Cons:**
- Additional dependency
- Slightly more complex implementation

### Option 3: Progressive Image Loading (Premium UX Enhancement)

For a polished experience with blur-up effect:

```tsx
import { LazyLoadImage } from 'react-lazy-load-image-component';
import 'react-lazy-load-image-component/src/effects/blur.css';

<LazyLoadImage
  src={`/assets/posts/${item.image}`}
  alt={item.title}
  effect="blur"
  width={400}
  height={200}
  style={{ objectFit: 'cover' }}
/>
```

**Pros:**
- Smooth blur-to-sharp transition
- Professional, polished feel
- Good perceived performance

**Cons:**
- Additional dependency
- Ideally needs low-res placeholder images for best effect

## Implementation Checklist

- [ ] Add `loading="lazy"` attribute to card images
- [ ] Add `decoding="async"` attribute
- [ ] Add explicit `width` and `height` attributes (400x200 based on current styling)
- [ ] Remove debug `console.log` statement (line 21-27) while in this file
- [ ] Test on Research page with Network tab throttled to "Slow 3G"
- [ ] Verify images load progressively as user scrolls
- [ ] Check for layout shift (CLS) with Lighthouse

## Additional Optimizations to Consider (Future)

1. **Image format optimization** - Serve WebP/AVIF with fallbacks
2. **Responsive images** - Use `srcset` for different viewport sizes
3. **Image CDN** - Consider Cloudinary/ImageKit for automatic optimization
4. **Skeleton loaders** - Show animated placeholders while images load
5. **Priority hints** - Use `fetchpriority="high"` for above-fold images

## Testing

1. Open Research page with DevTools Network tab open
2. Filter by "Img" to see image requests
3. With current implementation: All ~160 images load immediately
4. With lazy loading: Only visible images (~6-10) should load initially
5. Scroll down and verify additional images load progressively

## Browser Support

Native `loading="lazy"` is supported in:
- Chrome 77+ ✓
- Firefox 75+ ✓
- Safari 15.4+ ✓
- Edge 79+ ✓

Coverage: ~95% of users. Browsers without support will simply load images normally (graceful degradation).

